vps-ready <version> on Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie) (vm-other)
Audit: 13 passed, 12 failed (1 high, 5 medium, 6 low), 4 unknown. vps-ready can fix 11 of them.

? What will this server run?
  > Web apps or Docker: opens ports 80 and 443 (recommended: Docker is installed)
    Something else: only SSH is open
? Admin user for logging in: admin
  new account: logs in with 1 key copied from /root/.ssh/authorized_keys; sudo
  without a password.
  Root and password logins will be turned off, so this key is how you log in.
All other incoming ports are blocked.
Unchecked ports will be blocked.
? Open ports besides SSH (22, always open) (Space toggles, Enter continues)
  [x] 80/tcp
  [x] 443/tcp
  [x] 443/udp
? More ports to open, e.g. 8080/tcp or 53/udp (Enter for none): 
? Pick which modules to run yourself? (y/N) no
Caddy can serve your apps over HTTPS and get their certificates. Each domain
needs a DNS record for this server.
? Domain to serve, e.g. app.example.com (Enter for none): App.example.com
  "App.example.com" is not a domain name, e.g. app.example.com: lowercase
  letters, digits and `-`, in two or more labels separated by dots, with no
  wildcard
? Domain to serve, e.g. app.example.com (Enter for none): app.example.com
? Address of the app behind app.example.com, e.g. 127.0.0.1:8080: localhost:8080
  "localhost:8080": write 127.0.0.1:8080 instead; localhost may resolve to ::1,
  and Docker publishes ports on 127.0.0.1 only
? Address of the app behind app.example.com, e.g. 127.0.0.1:8080: 127.0.0.1:8080
? Another domain (Enter for no more): app.example.com
  app.example.com is already listed
? Another domain (Enter for no more): 

After you apply, you have 5 minutes to log in from a new terminal and confirm.
If you don't, every change is undone.

user.admin: Set up an admin account that logs in with an SSH key and uses sudo
  note: no `authorized_keys` given: copies 1 key from /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
  + create user admin                                                     partial
  + ~admin/.ssh/authorized_keys (mode 0600, admin:admin)                  reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1 @@
      +ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIGroot root@laptop
  + /etc/sudoers.d/90-vps-ready-admin (mode 0440, root:root)              reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (user.admin). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +admin ALL=(ALL:ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
ssh.server: Turn off root and password logins over SSH (fixes SSH-001, SSH-002, SSH-005)
  note: SSH sessions that are open now stay open; the new settings apply to new logins
  note: Port, ListenAddress, AllowGroups, AuthenticationMethods and the crypto settings stay as they are
  + /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-vps-ready.conf (mode 0644, root:root)       reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (ssh.server). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +# sshd uses the first value it reads for each keyword, and reads this file
      +# before 50-cloud-init.conf and the rest of sshd_config.
      +PermitRootLogin no
      +PasswordAuthentication no
      +X11Forwarding no
deploy.docker: Install Docker with logs capped and ports on loopback by default
  note: Docker is installed, but not from Docker's package: only /etc/docker/daemon.json is managed
  note: ports published without an address bind to 127.0.0.1 only, not to ::1: point a reverse proxy at 127.0.0.1:<port>, not localhost
  note: docker.service is not running; Docker reads the new daemon.json when it starts
  + directory /etc/docker (mode 0755, root:root)                          reversible
  + /etc/docker/daemon.json (mode 0644, root:root)                        reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
      +{
      +  "log-driver": "local",
      +  "log-opts": {
      +    "max-size": "10m",
      +    "max-file": "3"
      +  },
      +  "live-restore": true,
      +  "no-new-privileges": true,
      +  "userland-proxy": false,
      +  "ip": "127.0.0.1",
      +  "default-network-opts": {
      +    "bridge": {
      +      "com.docker.network.bridge.host_binding_ipv4": "127.0.0.1"
      +    }
      +  }
      +}
firewall.inbound: Drop incoming traffic that no rule allows (fixes FW-001)
  note: always accepts TCP 22, the current SSH ports (this session's, root's sshd listeners and `Port`), whatever `allow` says
  note: Docker is installed: input from docker0 and br-* is accepted, so containers can reach services on the host; a compromised container can reach all of them, as with no firewall
  + directory /etc/vps-ready (mode 0755, root:root)                       reversible
  + /etc/vps-ready/firewall.nft (mode 0644, root:root)                    reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (firewall.inbound). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +# vps-ready-firewall.service loads it. It replaces only the table
      +# inet vps_ready: other tables, such as Docker's or fail2ban's, stay.
      +table inet vps_ready {}
      +delete table inet vps_ready
      +table inet vps_ready {
      +  chain input {
      +    type filter hook input priority filter; policy drop;
      +    ct state established,related accept
      +    ct state invalid drop
      +    iif lo accept
      +    meta l4proto icmp accept
      +    # Neighbor discovery and path MTU: never block ICMPv6.
      +    meta l4proto icmpv6 accept
      +    # DHCPv4 replies that conntrack does not match (broadcast).
      +    udp sport 67 udp dport 68 accept
      +    # DHCPv6 client.
      +    ip6 saddr fe80::/10 udp dport 546 accept
      +    # Containers reaching services on the host.
      +    iifname "docker0" accept
      +    iifname "br-*" accept
      +    # The profile's `allow`, plus every current SSH port.
      +    tcp dport { 22, 80, 443 } accept
      +    udp dport { 443 } accept
      +  }
      +}
  + /etc/systemd/system/vps-ready-firewall.service (mode 0644, root:root)  reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (firewall.inbound). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +[Unit]
      +Description=vps-ready: inbound firewall (nftables table inet vps_ready)
      +DefaultDependencies=no
      +Wants=network-pre.target
      +After=nftables.service
      +Before=network-pre.target shutdown.target
      +Conflicts=shutdown.target
      +
      +[Service]
      +Type=oneshot
      +RemainAfterExit=yes
      +ExecStart=/usr/sbin/nft -f /etc/vps-ready/firewall.nft
      +ExecReload=/usr/sbin/nft -f /etc/vps-ready/firewall.nft
      +ExecStop=-/usr/sbin/nft delete table inet vps_ready
      +
      +[Install]
      +WantedBy=sysinit.target
  ~ enable and start vps-ready-firewall.service                           reversible
ssh.bruteforce: Ban addresses that keep failing to log in over SSH
  note: no SSH session found, so only localhost is exempt from bans
  note: if this installs fail2ban, rollback leaves it installed and enabled, as the package sets it up, so it runs the distro's own jails after a reboot; `systemctl disable --now fail2ban` turns it off
  + /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/90-vps-ready.local (mode 0644, root:root)        reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (ssh.bruteforce). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +[DEFAULT]
      +banaction = nftables
      +backend = systemd
      +
      +[sshd]
      +enabled = true
      +ignoreip = 127.0.0.1/8 ::1
  ~ enable and start fail2ban.service                                     reversible
kernel.sysctl: Harden kernel settings (fixes KRN-001, KRN-002, KRN-003, KRN-007, KRN-008, NET-002)
  + /etc/sysctl.d/90-vps-ready.conf (mode 0644, root:root)                reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (kernel.sysctl). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +kernel.kptr_restrict = 1
      +kernel.dmesg_restrict = 1
      +kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled = 2
      +fs.protected_fifos = 1
      +fs.protected_regular = 1
      +net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_source_route = 0
  ~ kernel.kptr_restrict 0 -> 1                                           reversible
  ~ kernel.dmesg_restrict 0 -> 1                                          reversible
  ~ kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled 0 -> 2                               reversible
  ~ fs.protected_fifos 0 -> 1                                             reversible
  ~ fs.protected_regular 0 -> 1                                           reversible
  ~ net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_source_route 1 -> 0                          reversible
updates.auto: Install security updates automatically (fixes UPD-001)
  + /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52vps-ready (mode 0644, root:root)                reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
      +APT::Periodic::Enable "1";
      +APT::Periodic::Update-Package-Lists "1";
      +APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade "1";
deploy.proxy: Serve sites over HTTPS with Caddy, proxied to their upstreams
